        [("extraversion_high", "oxytocin")] = "OXTR expression in NAc shell and ventral striatum potentiates crowd-driven affiliative reward cascades. Large-group bonding rituals release oxytocin surges that amplify interpersonal synchrony across temporoparietal junction mirror neuron circuits. In-group belonging operates as primary physiological reinforcer — party attendance and team collaboration generate sustained OXTR-mediated wellbeing that motivates further social approach.",
        [("extraversion_high", "oxytocin_h")] = "Hypothalamic paraventricular oxytocin release during physical social contact drives powerful approach motivation through ventral pallidum reward gating. Hugging, handshaking, and shoulder-touch activate magnocellular projections creating biological urgency for proximity. Group gatherings energize through oxytocin-dopamine synergy in mesolimbic circuitry — social touch as metabolic fuel rather than sensory overload.",
        [("extraversion_high", "endorphins")] = "Mu-opioid receptor activation from shared laughter in group settings and team celebration rituals. Beta-endorphin release from arcuate nucleus during social grooming, party atmosphere, and crowd excitement. Partner-conditioned MOR activation extends to broad social networks — the neurochemistry of belonging to large friend groups and community gatherings.",
        [("extraversion_high", "dopamine")] = "Mesolimbic VTA phasic bursting amplified by social novelty cues — new faces, party invitations, group attention. D2 receptor density in ventral striatum generates anticipatory wanting for upcoming gatherings and public performances. NAc shell activation codes social approach as primary reward prediction signal — being the center of attention triggers dopamine surges comparable to achievement reward.",
        [("extraversion_high", "testosterone")] = "Androgen receptor activation in amygdala drives assertive social dominance displays — commanding room attention, initiating conversations with strangers, competitive status signaling in group hierarchies. Testosterone-dopamine interaction in mesocortical circuits amplifies confidence for public speaking and leadership assertion.",
        [("extraversion_high", "enkephalins")] = "Delta-opioid DOR tone sustained by continuous social warmth — large friend networks and frequent group interaction maintain elevated baseline contentment. Proenkephalin expression in striatum tied to social network size and interaction frequency. Being surrounded by people generates steady hedonic satisfaction through DOR-mediated reward.",
        [("extraversion_high", "npy")] = "Y1 receptor anxiolytic buffering in amygdala eliminates social performance anxiety, enabling fearless crowd engagement and spontaneous public interaction. Robust NPY expression creates neurochemical foundation for social confidence — approaching strangers, networking events, and impromptu presentations feel effortless rather than threatening.",
        [("extraversion_high", "dhea")] = "DHEA:cortisol ratio strongly elevated during social performance contexts — neurosteroid buffering sustains high-energy group engagement without stress accumulation. Assertive social approach powered by anti-glucocorticoid resilience. Marathon social sessions metabolically supported by DHEA-mediated cortisol antagonism.",
        [("extraversion_high", "adrenaline")] = "Beta-adrenergic sympathetic arousal in crowd settings experienced as electrifying activation energy rather than threat response. Public speaking and group leadership trigger beta-1/beta-2 receptor activation that amplifies performance rather than impairing it. Social spotlight as exhilarating sympathetic surge.",

        // ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        // EXTRAVERSION LOW — reserved, quiet, solitary, energy-conserving
